  "replacements": [
    {
      "before": "<p style=\"margin-top:0;\">(a) Show that $g(x)=\\cos x$ maps $J=[\\cos1,1]$ into itself and is a contraction there with $L=\\sin1$. (b) From $x_0=1$, how many iterations does the a priori bound guarantee for $|x_k-x^\\ast|\\le10^{-6}$? (c) In fact $32$ suffice; explain the gap. (d) Evaluate the a posteriori bound at $k=10$, where $x_9=0.731404$, $x_{10}=0.744237$.</p>",
      "after": "<p style=\"margin-top:0;\">(a) Show that $g(x)=\\cos x$ maps $J=[\\cos1,1]$ into itself and is a contraction there with $L=\\sin1$. (b) From $x_0=1$, how many iterations does the a priori bound guarantee for $|x_k-x^\\ast|\\le10^{-6}$? (c) In fact $32$ suffice; explain the gap. (d) Evaluate the a posteriori bound at $k=10$, where $x_9\\approx0.731404$, $x_{10}\\approx0.744237$.</p>",
      "count": 1,
      "reason": "The actual ninth and tenth iterates have certified enclosures strictly different from these finite decimals; the values are nearest six-place approximations."
    },
    {
      "before": "(a) $\\cos$ decreases on $[0,\\pi/2]\\supseteq J$, so $\\cos(J)=[\\cos1,\\cos(\\cos1)]=[0.5403,0.8576]\\subseteq J$. By the mean value theorem $|\\cos x-\\cos y|=|\\sin\\xi|\\,|x-y|\\le\\sin1\\,|x-y|$ on $J$, so $L=0.8415$; $J$ is closed, and Banach gives the unique fixed point $x^\\ast=0.739085$. (b) $|x_1-x_0|=|0.5403-1|=0.4597$ and $1-L=0.1585$: $k\\ge\\ln\\big(0.4597/(0.1585\\cdot10^{-6})\\big)/\\ln(1/0.8415)=14.88/0.1726=86.2$, so $87$. (c) $L$ is the worst slope on $J$, but near $x^\\ast$ the slope is $\\sin x^\\ast=0.674$; with $|x_0-x^\\ast|=0.261$ this predicts $\\ln(0.261\\cdot10^6)/\\ln(1/0.674)=31.6$, i.e. $32$. The bound must hold for every start in $J$. (d) $\\frac{L}{1-L}|x_{10}-x_9|=5.31\\cdot0.012833=0.068$, against the true error $0.0052$ and the a priori bound $0.52$ at $k=10$.",
      "after": "(a) $\\cos$ decreases on $[0,\\pi/2]\\supseteq J$, so $\\cos(J)=[\\cos1,\\cos(\\cos1)]\\subseteq J$, with endpoints approximately $0.5403$ and $0.8576$. By the mean value theorem $|\\cos x-\\cos y|=|\\sin\\xi|\\,|x-y|\\le\\sin1\\,|x-y|$ on $J$, so $L=\\sin1\\approx0.8415$; $J$ is closed, and Banach gives the unique fixed point $x^\\ast\\approx0.739085$. (b) $|x_1-x_0|=1-\\cos1\\approx0.4597$ and $1-L=1-\\sin1\\approx0.1585$. The exact a priori certificate is equivalent to $k\\ge\\ln\\big((1-\\cos1)/((1-\\sin1)10^{-6})\\big)/\\ln(1/\\sin1)\\approx86.2$, so its first integer cutoff is $87$. For the rounded inputs, $\\ln\\big(0.4597/(0.1585\\cdot10^{-6})\\big)\\approx14.88$ and $\\ln(1/0.8415)\\approx0.1726$. (c) $L$ is the worst slope on $J$, but near $x^\\ast$ the slope is $\\sin x^\\ast\\approx0.674$; with $|x_0-x^\\ast|\\approx0.261$ the local-rate estimate $\\ln(0.261\\cdot10^6)/\\ln(1/0.674)\\approx31.6$ suggests $32$. A direct certified iteration verifies that $32$ suffice. The global bound must hold for every start in $J$. (d) $\\frac{L}{1-L}|x_{10}-x_9|\\approx5.31\\cdot0.012833\\approx0.068$, against the true error $\\approx0.0052$ and the a priori bound $\\approx0.52$ at $k=10$.",
      "count": 1,
      "reason": "All displayed finite cosine/sine/iterate/error/log values are rounded quantities. The exact a priori logarithmic cutoff must use unrounded cos1/sin1 rather than replace them by decimals. The near-fixed-point rate is an explanatory estimate; an independent true iteration enclosure proves that32 actually suffice."
    }
  ],
